From b6289e509f3188eabc82f2d6e25e3c253c159ebc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:12:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] FEX: frame credit resync (VKD3D_FEX_LATENCY_RESYNC)

The game's frame latency handle holds the credits for frames it may
start: SetMaximumFrameLatency(2) makes two, the game takes one before
each frame and vkd3d hands one back once that frame's present is done.
A frame started without taking one leaves a credit too many for good.
In the D2R logs of FEX builds 74 and 77 the game's wait on the handle
timed out after 1 s during a 1.6 s hitch at startup: from then on the
game ran a frame further ahead than it asked for, only vkd3d's own
latency of 3 in Present() held it, and frames took ~100 ms from start
to screen with no hold-back (~67 with two frames), ~50 at best.

Each hand-out now compares the game's Present() calls (read before the
hand-out) with the credits it took (handed out minus the count the
hand-out returns) and the hand-outs held back so far: a game exactly at
its latency is at -1 or 0, one a frame ahead at 0 or 1. When a window
of 32 hand-outs saw it ahead (all looks within one of each other, and
the game took a credit for at least 24 of them), the next hand-outs
are held back by as many: the game's next frame starts one frame later
once, then it is back at the latency it set. More than 8 such actions
in 60 s stop it for the swapchain (the game doesn't take one credit
per frame).

On unless VKD3D_FEX_LATENCY_RESYNC=0 while frame start pacing is on
(FEX sets that for D2R); 1 turns it on without. Windows builds only:
it needs ReleaseSemaphore's previous count. With the FEX timeline on,
a 'frame credits (resync)' line shows how far ahead the game was and
the hand-outs held back; the present note says whether it is on.
---
